1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,093
Why does my phone

2
00:00:01,093 --> 00:00:02,137
shut off at 20%?!

3
00:00:02,317 --> 00:00:04,607
Professor Poppleton here! Let's POP the mystery!

4
00:00:04,787 --> 00:00:07,031
Here's the twist. It still has charge.

5
00:00:07,031 --> 00:00:09,276
In cold tests on phones, they switched

6
00:00:09,276 --> 00:00:10,930
off still showing up to 33%!

7
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:14,004
Cold, old batteries and heavy

8
00:00:14,004 --> 00:00:15,689
use all play a part.

9
00:00:16,169 --> 00:00:18,260
Today's experiment: the gauge, the

10
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:19,490
cold, and the cliff.

11
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:21,642
Three clues. One very sneaky

12
00:00:21,642 --> 00:00:23,333
battery. Let's crack it!

13
00:00:23,913 --> 00:00:26,067
Guess first! What makes your phone

14
00:00:26,067 --> 00:00:28,284
switch itself off? The battery hits

15
00:00:28,284 --> 00:00:30,121
0%, the voltage sags too low,

16
00:00:30,121 --> 00:00:31,894
or the screen gets too cold?

17
00:00:34,994 --> 00:00:36,306
Lock in your answer. We'll

18
00:00:36,306 --> 00:00:37,315
test it in a moment.

19
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:39,647
So, experiment one. How does your

20
00:00:39,647 --> 00:00:41,387
phone even know its percentage?

21
00:00:41,567 --> 00:00:43,370
It can't peek inside the battery. There's

22
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:44,865
no fuel tank with a little needle!

23
00:00:45,045 --> 00:00:47,408
Instead, a tiny gauge chip measures

24
00:00:47,408 --> 00:00:50,580
three things: voltage, current and temperature.

25
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:52,947
Then software turns those readings into an

26
00:00:52,947 --> 00:00:55,134
estimate. Your percentage is a best guess!

27
00:00:55,314 --> 00:00:56,834
Think of guessing how full a bottle

28
00:00:56,834 --> 00:00:58,181
is, just by feeling its weight.

29
00:00:58,361 --> 00:01:00,072
Handy! But the chip only measures

30
00:01:00,072 --> 00:01:01,991
the outside, never the charge itself.

31
00:01:02,621 --> 00:01:05,396
And surprise switch-offs are common. In

32
00:01:05,396 --> 00:01:07,388
a survey of 121 phone users,

33
00:01:07,388 --> 00:01:08,953
6 in 10 had seen their

34
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:11,087
phone switch off unexpectedly.

35
00:01:11,267 --> 00:01:13,126
6 in 10! That's almost as many

36
00:01:13,126 --> 00:01:15,482
surprises as my hair gives the barber.

37
00:01:16,162 --> 00:01:17,710
But why would the cold matter?

38
00:01:17,710 --> 00:01:19,207
The charge is still in there.

39
00:01:19,387 --> 00:01:21,512
Inside, your battery moves lithium

40
00:01:21,512 --> 00:01:23,200
ions through a liquid, from

41
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,575
one side to the other.

42
00:01:24,755 --> 00:01:27,855
Lithium ions are tiny charged atoms. They

43
00:01:27,855 --> 00:01:29,972
are the charge, on the move.

44
00:01:30,152 --> 00:01:32,030
In the cold, that liquid gets

45
00:01:32,030 --> 00:01:34,232
thicker, like honey in the fridge.

46
00:01:34,232 --> 00:01:35,851
The ions slow right down.

47
00:01:36,031 --> 00:01:37,513
Picture a crowd at a

48
00:01:37,513 --> 00:01:40,255
doorway. Warm crowd: everyone streams

49
00:01:40,255 --> 00:01:43,144
through. Cold crowd: everyone shuffles.

50
00:01:43,774 --> 00:01:47,628
That slowdown is called resistance. In tests

51
00:01:47,628 --> 00:01:51,132
on one battery cell, resistance at -25°C

52
00:01:51,132 --> 00:01:53,672
was 7.6x higher than at 50°C.

53
00:01:53,852 --> 00:01:55,956
Lab tests show that below about

54
00:01:55,956 --> 00:01:57,992
-10°C, the ions' jump into the

55
00:01:57,992 --> 00:02:00,436
electrodes becomes the main blocker.

56
00:02:01,216 --> 00:02:03,467
POP QUIZ! In tests on one cell,

57
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:06,227
how much did resistance grow after 800

58
00:02:06,227 --> 00:02:08,841
charge cycles? About 2x, 6x, or 20x?

59
00:02:12,291 --> 00:02:14,091
About 6x, by our maths!

60
00:02:14,271 --> 00:02:15,965
So an old battery is like a

61
00:02:15,965 --> 00:02:18,786
crowd squeezing through a much narrower door.

62
00:02:18,967 --> 00:02:20,811
Click on subscribe, so your brain

63
00:02:20,811 --> 00:02:22,040
can be as big as mine!

64
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,193
Therefore, here's the real switch. Your phone

65
00:02:25,193 --> 00:02:27,302
needs a minimum voltage to keep running.

66
00:02:27,482 --> 00:02:29,617
In tests on one phone, it kept

67
00:02:29,617 --> 00:02:32,250
going until the battery fell to about

68
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:34,242
3.4 volts. Then, click. Off.

69
00:02:34,422 --> 00:02:36,841
That's how phones in those cold tests

70
00:02:36,841 --> 00:02:39,259
switched off still showing up to 33%.

71
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:41,481
Plenty of charge, not enough push!

72
00:02:42,261 --> 00:02:43,713
Remember your guess? What really

73
00:02:43,713 --> 00:02:44,802
switches your phone off?

74
00:02:45,432 --> 00:02:46,701
The voltage sags too

75
00:02:46,701 --> 00:02:48,225
low! Not the percentage.

76
00:02:48,405 --> 00:02:50,631
And here's the best part. In

77
00:02:50,631 --> 00:02:52,778
a lab test, a phone chilled

78
00:02:52,778 --> 00:02:54,449
to -5°C switched off.

79
00:02:55,079 --> 00:02:57,504
Once it warmed up, it switched back

80
00:02:57,504 --> 00:02:59,792
on and ran about 18 more minutes!

81
00:02:59,972 --> 00:03:01,124
The charge was hiding in

82
00:03:01,124 --> 00:03:02,132
there the whole time!

83
00:03:03,012 --> 00:03:05,072
But voltage isn't fixed. When your

84
00:03:05,072 --> 00:03:07,192
phone suddenly works hard, it gulps

85
00:03:07,192 --> 00:03:08,343
a burst of current.

86
00:03:08,523 --> 00:03:09,928
That burst drags the voltage

87
00:03:09,928 --> 00:03:11,083
down for a moment. More

88
00:03:11,083 --> 00:03:12,589
resistance means a deeper dip.

89
00:03:12,769 --> 00:03:15,330
An old, nearly flat battery is

90
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:17,634
worse. In tests on one aged

91
00:03:17,634 --> 00:03:20,878
cell, its resistance could double near

92
00:03:20,878 --> 00:03:23,012
10% charge, by our maths.

93
00:03:23,192 --> 00:03:25,003
So: cold, old and low, plus

94
00:03:25,003 --> 00:03:26,947
one big gulp, and the voltage

95
00:03:26,947 --> 00:03:28,288
tips over the cliff.

96
00:03:29,068 --> 00:03:30,969
Guess first! In a lab test,

97
00:03:30,969 --> 00:03:33,012
how much deeper was a chilled

98
00:03:33,012 --> 00:03:35,335
phone's voltage dip? 2 times, 3.5

99
00:03:35,335 --> 00:03:36,673
times, or 10 times?

100
00:03:39,773 --> 00:03:42,300
Got it? Let's dive in and measure.

101
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:45,180
Science deep dive! The rule behind

102
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:46,803
the cliff is Ohm's law.

103
00:03:46,983 --> 00:03:49,231
The voltage you lose equals the

104
00:03:49,231 --> 00:03:52,349
current times the resistance. Double either

105
00:03:52,349 --> 00:03:53,872
one, double the loss.

106
00:03:54,052 --> 00:03:56,179
Picture a garden hose. Squeeze it, and

107
00:03:56,179 --> 00:03:57,802
the push at the nozzle drops.

108
00:03:58,432 --> 00:04:00,842
Now the whoa number. In that

109
00:04:00,842 --> 00:04:03,423
lab test, the phone chilled to

110
00:04:03,423 --> 00:04:06,263
-5°C, about 23°F, lost 0.49 volts

111
00:04:06,263 --> 00:04:08,759
right before it switched off.

112
00:04:08,939 --> 00:04:12,285
Warmed to room temperature, the same phone

113
00:04:12,285 --> 00:04:14,994
lost just 0.14 volts. The cold dip

114
00:04:14,994 --> 00:04:17,384
was 3.5x bigger, by our maths!

115
00:04:17,564 --> 00:04:20,174
Steady, Poppleton, steady... no, I'm too excited!

116
00:04:21,154 --> 00:04:22,317
So here's the whole machine.

117
00:04:22,497 --> 00:04:24,688
Cold, age and low charge raise

118
00:04:24,688 --> 00:04:27,171
the resistance. A burst of current

119
00:04:27,171 --> 00:04:29,435
meets it, and the voltage dips.

120
00:04:29,615 --> 00:04:31,932
Dip under about 3.4 volts, and the

121
00:04:31,932 --> 00:04:34,999
phone switches off, with charge still inside.

122
00:04:35,779 --> 00:04:37,262
THE BIG POP: at 20%, your

123
00:04:37,262 --> 00:04:39,336
phone is still charged. The voltage

124
00:04:39,336 --> 00:04:41,115
dipped below its line, and the

125
00:04:41,115 --> 00:04:42,716
cold makes that dip deeper.

126
00:04:42,896 --> 00:04:44,723
Your percentage isn't lying. It just

127
00:04:44,723 --> 00:04:45,991
can't see the dip coming.

128
00:04:46,171 --> 00:04:47,384
The charge was in there all

129
00:04:47,384 --> 00:04:48,955
along, waiting for a little warmth.

130
00:04:50,135 --> 00:04:51,746
Has your phone ever switched off

131
00:04:51,746 --> 00:04:53,206
with charge left? Tell me the

132
00:04:53,206 --> 00:04:54,566
percentage in the comments.

133
